 * Name: preview_convert
 *
 * Description:
 *   One frame: centre-crop, 3:1 nearest-neighbour downscale and packed
 *   4:2:2 YUV to RGB565, straight from the capture buffer into the first
 *   framebuffer.  BT.601 coefficients in 8-bit fixed point; the shifts are
 *   chosen so the whole conversion stays in 32-bit integer math.
 *
 *   Byte order is UYVY: each 4-byte group is U Y0 V Y1, so a source x maps
 *   to group (x >> 1) and picks Y0 (byte 1) or Y1 (byte 3) by x's parity,
 *   with U (byte 0) and V (byte 2) shared by both pixels.
 *
 *   How that was settled (2026-08-10, second attempt).  This decoded UYVY
 *   originally, was changed to YUYV, and is now back to UYVY.  The round
 *   trip is worth recording because the deciding evidence only appeared at
 *   the end:
 *
 *   - The change to YUYV cited `ffmpeg -pix_fmt yuyv422` from
 *     docs/main/2026-08-10-capture-success-and-prior-doc-limitations.md
 *     §4.1 as if it were a confirmed result.  It was not: §4.2 of that same
 *     document lists "YUYV 字节序" among the things *not yet verified*.  A
 *     command someone wrote down is not a measurement.
 *
 *   - The first-frame hexdump in that document is
 *     `7f 33 80 36 7f 32 80 32 7f 36 81 36 ...`.  Byte 0 of every group is
 *     a constant 0x7f while byte 2 varies (0x80 0x80 0x81 0x87 0x88).  Read
 *     as UYVY that is U == 127 and V == 128..136, i.e. chroma sitting on
 *     the neutral point -- a dark, nearly colourless region, which is an
 *     ordinary way for the top-left of a frame to look.  Read as YUYV it is
 *     chroma at 51/53, far off neutral, i.e. a mid-grey but violently green
 *     region.  Both readings are self-consistent, so these 32 bytes cannot
 *     settle the question at all.
 *
 *   - Photographs of the panel running the YUYV decode settled it: the
 *     picture was geometrically correct and clearly recognisable, but every
 *     pixel was either saturated green or magenta.  That is exactly what
 *     this arithmetic predicts when U is fed to the luma term and Y to the
 *     chroma terms: with y_code == (U-16)*298 ≈ 33376 constant and
 *     u_code == v_code == Y-128 == t,
 *
 *         r = (33376 + 409t) >> 8 = 130 + 1.6t
 *         g = (33376 - 308t) >> 8 = 130 - 1.2t
 *         b = (33376 + 516t) >> 8 = 130 + 2.0t
 *
 *     so t < 0 (dark) drives green and t > 0 (bright) drives magenta, while
 *     luma structure survives because t still carries it.  Green/magenta
 *     bipolarity with intact structure is therefore the *signature* of a
 *     swapped luma/chroma, and distinguishes it from a byte-order fault
 *     (which would not preserve structure this cleanly).
 *
 *   Because this has now flipped twice, the decode order is selectable at
 *   run time so it can be confirmed on the glass in one flash rather than
 *   argued about.
 *
 *   Saturation: with UYVY decoding correct the picture was accurate but
 *   washed out, matching the narrow chroma excursion visible in the hexdump
 *   above (+-8 around neutral where a natural scene gives +-40).  g_sat
 *   scales the chroma difference before the matrix; the default of 150%
 *   compensates without clipping typical content.  g_gain, g_full_range and
 *   g_uv_swap are provided for the same reason -- tuning colour needs the
 *   real panel in the loop.
 *
 ****************************************************************************/

/* One source row's worth of the cropped region, staged in SRAM.
 *
 * Why this exists: the capture buffer is in PSRAM, mapped non-cacheable, and
 * a single CPU read of it costs on the order of microseconds.  Measured:
 * converting one 160x160 frame with per-pixel reads straight out of PSRAM
 * took 194ms, i.e. ~7.6us per output pixel -- about 2400 CPU cycles at
 * 320MHz for what is a load plus a dozen arithmetic instructions.  Reducing
 * the number of reads per pixel from three to one changed nothing (180ms ->
 * 194ms, inside the noise), which is what ruled out access *count* and
 * pointed at per-access *latency*.
 *
 * A bulk memcpy, by contrast, is sequential and lets the PSRAM controller
 * burst.  So each needed row is copied once and then read repeatedly out of
 * SRAM.
 *
 * Only the cropped span is staged: the centre 480 of 640 columns is quad
 * indices CROP_X/2 .. (CROP_X+CROP_SIZE)/2, i.e. 240 quads = 960 bytes,
 * instead of the full 1280-byte row.  Static rather than on the stack
 * because this app's STACKSIZE is 4096.
 */

#define ROW_QUADS   (CROP_SIZE / 2)            /* 240 */
#define ROW_BYTES   (ROW_QUADS * 4)            /* 960 */
#define ROW_Q0      (CROP_X / 2)               /* first quad index staged */

static uint32_t g_rowbuf[ROW_QUADS];

